      "text": "VOLUME CONJECTURE AND QUANTUM HYPERBOLIC INVARIANTS: THE FIGURE EIGHT KNOT COMPLEMENT — We compute the real part of the semi-classical limit of the se quence of quantum hyperbolic invariants (QHI) of\n\n**VOLUME CONJECTURE AND QUANTUM HYPERBOLIC INVARIANTS: THE FIGURE EIGHT KNOT COMPLEMENT**\n\n*STÉPHANE BASEILHAC, FATHI BEN ARIBI*\n\n66 pp, 78 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We compute the real part of the semi-classical limit of the se quence of quantum hyperbolic invariants (QHI) of the ﬁgure-eight knot comple ment M . We show that it is rigid, in the sense that it does not depend on the choice of holonomy r epresentation of M , and it is either 0 or equal to the hyperbolic volume of M divided by 2 π, depending on a parity condition satisﬁed by logarithms of the holonomy eigenvalu es on the canonical longitude, where the logarithms are parameters of the QHI of M . Along the way we also survey some relevant general features of the QHI.\n\n**Outline.** 1 Introduction; 2 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 1.1 Main result. In this paper we compute the ; 4 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 1.2 T riangulation-version of Theorem 1.1. Aga; 6 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 1.3 Structure of the proof of Theorem 1.1. Let; 2 Hred; 8 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 2 Vol(M ) and; 2 Geometric setup; 2.1 The quantum gluing varieties. These form t; 2.1.1 Quantum 3-simplices. (see [7], Section 3; 10 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 2.1.2 Gluing varieties and the holonomy map. I; 12 ST ´EPHANE BASEILHAC 1, F ATHI BEN ARIBI2; 2.2 By means of the formula (6) relating di; 2.2 W eights. The structure of the set of syst\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem 1.1: For every character ρ ∈Xhyp(M ) and charge weight kc, the following holds:\n- Theorem 1.1: seemingly reﬂects that the QHI could detect lift s to SL(2, C) of PSL (2, C)-\n- Theorem 1.2: Let (uN )N and (vN )N be sequences\n- Theorem 2.6: Let w ∈ G(T,b ). For any classes h2 ∈ H 1(M ; Z/2Z) and lκ ∈ H 1(∂ ¯M ; C)\n- Theorem 2.7: The map πN has degree Nn+p.\n- Theorem 8.44: ]). We call Xhyp(M ) the geometric component of X(M ).",
